September 30, 1928, exactly 90 years ago, British bacteriologist Aleksandr Fleming announced an unprecedented discovery.
Penicillin's discovery marked the beginning of the era of modern anti-strategy, brought Fleming world fame and made him a Nobel laureate.
By the way, excreating penicillin from mold mushrooms wasn't Fleming's first discovery.
A few years earlier, he, being a cold, sow mucus from his own nasal on a petriet cup (this is such a glass lid that experiments with the bacterium are carried out in a few days) and a few days later found that the bacteria there had been destroyed.
Almost by the same thing he accidentally discovered penicillin.
Fleming's name was included in the list of the 100 most outstanding personalities in the history of humankind.
Its discovery, according to many scientists, changed the course of history.
With the help of penicillin medicine began to cope with the majority of the most ancient diseases, previously considered incurable - tuberculosis, syphilis, gangre ...
When, soon after receiving the Nobel Prize, Fleming arrived in the French capital in 1945, Parisian newspapers wrote: "To defeat fascism and the liberation of France, he did more than dozens of divisions."
According to experts, Fleming's discovery of the first antibiotic in the world saved the life of more than 200 million inhabitants of the Earth.
Aleksandr Fleming died at his home in London in 1955 from a heartfelt baceat.
He was cremated, and a week later his ashes were buried in the famous St. Pavel Cathedral in London.
Where, by the way, our and his wife paid attention to the vocabulary Russian-speaking guide.
And a few years a few-thought the statue of Alexander Fleming in full growth, we were able to observe at the most unexpected place for this in the capital of the capital of Spain.
There, close to the main arena to perform the bullfight of Plaza de Toros-de-Vétas, the most famous Spanish matadars erected a monument to a person whose efforts were saved, in their opinion, the lives of hundreds of outstanding masters of correcite.
By the way, in my judging this very interesting porcelain work I was found there, in Madrid, but at other times and not at all about it at all.
