Kosachev: At the 73-th session of the General Assembly of the United Nations, the topic of Russia in a negligible vein was practically absent
The Chairman of the Federation Council Committee on international Affairs Konstantin Kosachev considers the 73-th session of the UN General Assembly successful for Russia and does not preclude that it will be able to become a pivotal in the perception of the Russian Federation as an integral participant in solving global global problems.
“The uniqueness of the current 73-th session, in my opinion, lies in the fact that the rhetoric, to which we are almost accustomed in recent years in relation to Russia, has now much more muffled,” the senator said in an interview published on Sunday by Rossiyskaya Gazeta.
He explained that in the previous years the Russian Federation was sometimes “indicated as one of the three main threats to humanity, then, along with Ebola, along with climate change or terror, Russia was in this series, at least in the exproposition of a group of Western countries, which in this regard was expressed quite unanimously.”
At the 73-th session, Kosachev stressed, "the topic of Russia in a negative way was practically absent."
“If you carefully analyze the speech of the President of the United States, the President of France, the speech of the Minister of foreign Affairs of Germany, I now do not take Ukraine, Georgia, or the Britons, in any case, the choir did not exactly,” the politician said, noticing that “Russia was no longer a certain wall that is being coached with a tennis ball.”
The head of the profile committee of the Federation Council does not exclude that this testifies to a trend, which, in his opinion, “will highlight the current session [of the United Nations General Assembly], although this trend may be fully manifested in a year, in two, three”.
“I do not exclude that the current session in some sense can be a pivot,” Kosachev admitted, explaining that “there are too many examples of how to move forward in international affairs with the involvement of Russia.”
As an example, he cited the situation in Syria, about Iran and North Korea.
“Unfortunately yet, this is not Ukraine, but I have no quarrels that the Russian vision of the Ukrainian crisis will also be in demand over time,” the parliamentarian added.
This is due, he argues that there is an “adequate, sensible perception of Russia as not just possible – an inalienable participant in solving the global problems of our time”.
“I don’t want to make a mistake, but I have a feeling that we’re going through a dot somewhere where the movement will no longer turn back, it’s moving forward,” he said.
Kosachev called another "interesting and important feeling" in this context "a query for a query for the Russian vision of how the modern world should develop."
“I was attending a lot of meetings between the head of the Russian delegation [at the session of the General Assembly] the Minister of Foreign Affairs Sergey Lavrov, I held my own meetings, and it is with great pleasure to see that if a year or two or five years ago the Russian opinion, the Russian vision of what is happening in the world, by and large, no one was interested, now the situation is different”, – said the Chairman of the Committee of the Federation Department.
In his opinion, this is conditioned by the fact that "the so-called new liberal order, which is personified with the West, does not have the answers to questions on how to solve the world's problems due to the obvious shortage of ideas in the global space, a lack of ideas about how to solve the problems of the Middle East, the problems of terrorism, the problems of non-proliferation of nuclear weapons, the problems of migrating."
“And here the demand for the Russian vision is becoming more and more obvious,” Kosachev expressed his confidence, stressing that he considers in this sense “a modern session successful for the work of the Russian delegation, successful for Russia.”
