The first vice-speaker of the Verkhovna Rada Iryna Gerashchenko apologized to the convicts in the Russian Federation for “espionage” Ukrainian journalist Roman Sushchenko for the fact that he still has not been able to free himself from his dungeons.
About this she said in a video message to the political prisoner, recorded on the eve of the second anniversary of Sushchenko’s FSB illegal arrest.
According to her, in these days under the walls of the Russian Embassy in Paris there is an action, the participants of which announced a frantic protest with the requirement to vacate the prisoners of the Kremlin.
Every day the French journalists, politicians, cultural figures, among them there are people who are well-knowledging you, starve, supporting Oleg Sentsov, other political prisoners of the Kremlin, and, of course, you.
There is not a single international meeting, not a single conversation, not a single interview, where the President of Ukraine, the Ukrainian authorities, we did not demanded to put pressure on the Kremlin, to put pressure on Putin with one demand - the releasing Oleg, the releasing Roman, the releasing of all political prisoners of the Kremlin and those guys who are illegally detained in the occupied territories of the Donbass and the Crimea," Gerashchenko said.
She also expressed confidence that the tests will temper every Ukrainian, making us “sweaker, tougher in the protection of the national interests of Ukraine, in the defense of every Ukrainian, in the understanding of what is good and what evil is.”
Ukrinform correspondent Roman Sushchetko was detained by Russian security forces on September 30, 2016 at the Moscow airport.
On October 7, he was accused of espionage at the General Intelligence Directorate of the Ministry of Defense of Ukraine.
On June 4, 2018, the Moskovsky city court sentenced Sushchenko to 12 years in a strict-regime colony, and the Supreme Court of Russia recognized this verdict as lawful.
The Russian jailers granted the last request of the Ukrainian before his transfer to the colony.
