AfD became the second most popular in Germany
Anti-immigrant "Alternative for Germany" breaks another record of popularity, and Angela Merkel's ratings are falling.
It seems that the inhabitants of Germany began to seriously get tired of the immigrant policy of Angela Maech, thanks to which the country became a cohabitant target for millions of refugees from the Middle East.
This is most clearly indicated by the latest polls, according to which the anti-immigrant party “Alternative for Germany” is becoming increasingly popular, and the positions of the ruling bloc of Merkel and Seehofer are weakening.
This week AfD broke another record, rising to the second place in the ranking of folk sympathies.
The right-wing Conservative, with 17 per cent of popularity, managed to bypass the Social Democrats, who have about 16 per cent.
The cleavage of a one-percentage point can be attributed to a statistical error, but at the same time it should not be noteworthy the prevailing fact that the AfD created in 2013 from a small group of economists-Euroscoptists has managed to turn into a serious political force.
Leadership in the overall standings still continues to hold the ruling CSU/CDF bloc, which has about 30 percent.
However, there is a tendency to loss of glasses - back in April the support of Angela Merkel's bloc was four percentage points higher.
The personal rating of the German chancellor is also declining, which leads the German government for the fourth consecutive term.
Today, Angela Merkel’s support for 48 per cent of the Germans, which is 7 percent lower than last year.
It should be added that after the Social-Democrats are “Green” (15%), Left (11%) and Liberals (10%).
EvonquY "Alternative"
The “Alternative for Germany party” was created in September 2012 on the basis of the “Alternative Choice 2013”, which included immigrants from the Christian-Democratic Union.
Initially, the group initially stood in opposition to the government, but at first dealt only with economic issues, including the problems of the euro and cooperation within the EU.
At the same time, the group spoke outagad it against the “responsification” by Germany of weak EU economies, it did not preclude the possibility of a return to the German mark and a complete country’s withdrawal from the European Union.
Against the background of the deepening of the immigrant crisis, the AfD political platform has widened.
In 2013, the group entered a full-fledged batch, which occupied a niche of Euroskeptics and began to harshly criticize the government for the failure of immigration policy.
In the last parliamentary elections in 2017, the AfD managed to break into the Bundestag for for the first time, having received 94 deputy seats (out of 709).
There are two representatives of “Alternative for Germany” will receive seats in the European Parliament.
