The United States thought about the naval blockade of Russia because of the supply of oil and gas
In the U.S. can start the naval blockade of Russia in order to gain an advantage in the global energy market.
The U.S. authorities are so zealous in the struggle for success in the global hydrocarbon market that it is ready to resort to a naval blockade of Russia, which remains one of the world's main energy suppliers.
About the possibility of a naval smuggle on the eloquence told the head of the Ministry of internal Affairs of the United States Ryan Zinke.
Contrary to world jurisprudence, the U.S. Ministry of Internal Affairs performs non-policies, but civilian functions, including a policy in the field of natural resources.
Therefore, Zinke's statement was quite in the competence of the agency headed by him, although it was not devoid of political color.
Speaking at an industrial event in Pittsburgh, Zinke said that thanks to the U.S. naval forces control maritime routes in the Middle East and can block them if necessary.
From the words of Zinke it follows that the need may be associated with Washington’s desire to prevent Russian supplies to the global energy market.
Zinke recalled that Russia still receives a lion’s share of its revenues from deliveries to the global oil and gas market.
The desire to distribute energy supplies in the Middle East as in Eastern Europe was the main motivation of Moscow’s revitalization in this region, Zinke said.
The Minister made it clear that the U.S. still hopes to secure leadership in the global energy market, becoming the largest supplier of oil and gas.
To do this, Washington will have to squeeze both Iran and the maritime blockade of trade routes is an acceptable measure on this path, Zinke said.
