In the former Yugoslav Republic of Macedonia there is a referendum on the accession to the EU and NATO, subject to a change in the name of the country.
Referendum is intended to resolve a 27-year-old dispute with neighboring Greece about the name of the country, which coincides with the name of the neighboring Greek province.
Prime Minister Zoran Zaev was one of the first to vote, an terrific supporter of the renaissance of the republic to North Macedonia.
Preparing for a referendum is being monitored by international organizations today.
The head of the monitoring mission of the Office for Democratic Institutions and Human Rights, Jan Petersen, told Euronews how the work of observers is organized:
Approximately 100 teams moved around the country to observe the whole process today, from the opening of polling stations to their cloff, then for counting votes and drawing up protocols.
For a month in the country there were 10 of our teams, who monitored preparations for the vote, the work of the media, the observance of the law on the financing of election campaigns and the organization of a referendum.
Fei Dulgeri: "About 500 foreign observers are closely monitoring the course of the vote.
Observer groups record in detail what is happening and communicate with members of the election commissions in order to then set them out in the final report.”
The interpreter compared the mentality of the Russians and the German
The mentality of the Russian and German peoples is different, but a few differences between them do not play a great role for mutual similarity.
About it on the International Translator's Day, September 30, said professional synchronized interpreter Burbel Sack.
She has been engaged in translated from Russian and English for more than 30 years.
“The Germans like to calmly plan events, and the Russians, as they say in the proverbire, “clean themselves for a long time, and quickly go.”
But it seems to me that for mutual understanding between the peoples and for the work of an interpreter, this does not play a significant role, the expert said in a comment to RIA Novosti.
According to the interpreter, in order to understand that the two countries can not do without each other, “it is enough to know the history”.
And in the future, Sachke noted, there is no point in ignoring Russia.
Russia is a natural part of Europe, there is no point in trying to ignite it.
We need Russian gas, oil, there are a lot of things that determine our common destiny,” she said.
A day earlier, German Chancellor Angela Merkel said that the country is trying to “tie into relations” with Russia and support them.
According to her, the Nord Flood-2 gas pipeline project is especially important for Berlin mania, which is being structured from the coast of Russia to Germany along the bottom of the Baltic Sea.
