The main Macedonian Oppontrist Refuses to Vote in Referendum
In Macedonia on Sunday, September 30, there is a referendum on an intergovernmental treaty with Greece on the renaissance of the Republic of Macedonia.
Citizens are invited to answer a single question: “Do you support EU-NATO membership by accepting an agreement between the Republic of Madeodion and the Greek Republic?”
Nevertheless, the leader of the leader of the opposition party “Inner Macedonian Revolutionary Organization – the Democratic Party for the Macedonian National Unitahood” (VMRO-DPMNE) Christian Mitzkoi said that there would be no voting in a referendum.
As explained by the press Secretary of the party Naum Stoilkovsky, Mitsky decided to abstain from the vote in order to express his disagreement with the agreement, which he considers “detrimentary”.
According to the press Secretary, the current referendum decides on renaming the country and nothing more, while the party’s strategic goal is to achieve membership in NATO and the EU.
A week ago, Macedonian President George Ivanov also refused to participate in the referendum on September 30.
He explained that the name for the country he chose 27 years ago in the referendum on independence. The Macedonian leader considers the renaming of the country a negative factor.
“A new semi-sovereign state is being created with a new name,” Ivanov said.
The agreement on a new official naming of the former Yugoslav republic - the Republic of North Macedonia - was signed by the heads of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs of Macedonia and Greece on June 17 this year during a solemn ceremonial ceremony on the shore of Lake Prespa, on which the border between the two countries takes place.
This agreement should resolve a long-term dispute about the name of the country, as well as the Skopje route to NATO and the EU, reminds TASS.
